CVE-2026-42588: Harden ActiveMQ web console and Jolokia access by default

Backport of upstream commits be8415f248d311188a37d5e51b508acc80a0fb2d and
df95bf8f53e293b83d4b323ad3eb76158626d242 (apache/activemq 6.2.x) to 6.1.8.

The default sample configuration exposed the Jolokia HTTP-JMX bridge and web
console broadly, allowing an authenticated (default admin=admin) or CSRF/SSRF
attacker to invoke privileged broker JMX operations such as
Broker.addNetworkConnector / addConnector, leading to remote code execution
and lateral movement.

This config hardening:
  * jolokia-access.xml: restrict Jolokia to HTTP POST only and explicitly deny
    destructive/privileged broker MBean operations (addNetworkConnector,
    addConnector, terminateJVM, stop/restart, destination and message mutation,
    NetworkConnector credential exposure) and known JMX RCE/introspection
    surfaces (javax.management.loading MLet, JMImplementation, java.lang Runtime
    /Memory/ClassLoading, java.util.logging).
  * jetty.xml: gate /api/jolokia/* behind the admins-only security constraint,
    wrap the web console handlers in an InetAccessHandler restricted to loopback
    by default, and add hardening response headers.
  * activemq.xml: enable only the openwire connector by default, add an id to
    the broker bean (referenced by jetty.xml invokeStart depends-on), and ship a
    commented-out JAAS auth / authorization / audit plugins template.

Note: upstream commit 3a2397d4 (adds "masterslave" to
BrokerView.DENIED_TRANSPORT_SCHEMES) is intentionally not backported: the
6.1.8 target does not contain the URL-scheme deny-list validation feature that
commit extends, so it has no applicable context. The addNetworkConnector RCE
path is instead closed at the Jolokia access-control layer above.

